  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Warmer and more humid temperature build starting during the second \nhalf of the week. \n\nCooler temperatures through the middle of the week associated with a \ntrough will give way to increasing humidity and warmer temperatures \nstarting Wednesday afternoon. Increased temperatures and humidity \nwill last through the start of next week. \n\nTemperatures from earlier in the week in the 70s will warm to the \nupper 70s to mid 80s later in the week and into the weekend. \nSoutherly return flow on the backside of a departing sfc high will \nhelp induce WAA and start to push increased moisture into the \nregion. A passing warm front later Wednesday into Thursday will \nfurther increase temperatures and moisture across the area as the \nwarm sector of a passing sfc low becomes established over the \nregion. The cold front associated with the system will cross the \narea late Thursday into Friday morning. A drop in temperatures by a \nfew degrees for Friday/Saturday will be possible behind the front. \nBut temperatures and humidity will rebound and increase once\nagain. \n\n-------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Showers with strong to isolated severe thunderstorms\n are expected for Thursday. \n\nA pre-frontal trough tracking across the area ahead of the \napproaching cold front will cross the area during the afternoon \nhours. Currently the outlook from SPC is for a Marginal Risk for \nsevere thunderstorms across the entire area. Current guidance brings \nsomewhat favorable shear values of around 30 knots along with \ninstability of around 1,000-1,500 J/kg. Uncertainty among guidance \nfor the amount of cloud cover still remains as of the most recent \nmodel runs. Damaging winds with a lower chance for hail remains the \nprimary hazard. \n\nIncreased PWat values to around 1.50\" will also help increase the \npotential for heavy rainfall with thunderstorms that develop. The \neastern half of the area still remain in a Marginal Risk for \nexcessive runoff from WPC.",
